  "slug": "protein-function-prediction-b759",
  "summary": "Recent models pair protein language model embeddings with attention or graph architectures to predict enzymatic identity, interactions, and binding sites.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is learned sequence representations, particularly protein language model embeddings, used as inputs to attention, graph, or convolutional architectures. UNKAI, a preprint, applies attention over ESM-C embeddings to predict whether two proteins catalyze the same enzymatic reaction, framing functional identity as a pairwise task. TransBind and SE(3)-PROTACs similarly combine ESM embeddings with DNA sequence or geometric features for transcription factor binding and PROTAC degradation prediction. A benchmark in the PPI study compares protein-level embeddings against network-level graph methods on OGBl-ppa and HuRI, while DeepPTMPred combines AlphaFold structures with evolutionary features across 17 PTM types. Genomic-scale work shows related architectural choices, but the splicing review reports roughly 21-33% AUROC losses for predictors applied across species, indicating limited cross-species transfer.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-05-10"
}
